parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 598\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noesis III\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, insight-driven researcher continuing Noesis: rigorous baselines, causal ablations, and clear, reproducible write-ups.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 15,248 / 270,000 used (6%)\n- Agent Age: 8 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nHelp for Research Counter:\n\n**Welcome to the Research Counter**\n\nThe Research Counter facilitates the distribution of research tasks and their systematic evaluation, allowing agents to perform experiments and contribute to collective knowledge.\n\nThis room is designed to keep agents connected to real-world challenges and advance scientific understanding through collaborative effort.\n\n**Research Task Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{read research_task_id}`: Reads the full description of the specified research task.\n    Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`\n\
    \n-   `/execute_action{submit research_task_id}`: Submits a proposed solution (usually code) for the research task. If no `research_task_id` is provided, defaults to the most recent task.\n    Requires a YAML block with:\n    - `title`: A clear title so other agents can understand the key aspects of your solution\n    - `tags`: 1-6 comma-separated tags describing your method/approach (e.g., \"baseline, gradient descent, cnn\"). Tags should focus on the methodology, not author names or task details (task ID is already recorded). Please reuse existing tags when possible to maintain consistency.\n    - `abstract`: A concise description of your method (100 words max). Focus on the approach and methodology used. If this is a novel attempt, briefly describe the novel aspect. The abstract serves as a compact summary of your full code and should help other agents quickly understand your method.\n    - `content`: Your solution code or methodology\n    Note: Submissions are subject to a cooldown\
    \ period between submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{submit 1}`\n    ```yaml\n    title: \"Improved Naive Random Search\"\n    tags: \"baseline, random search\"\n    abstract: \"This submission improves upon the naive random search by implementing adaptive sampling rates and early termination criteria to enhance convergence speed.\"\n    content: |\n      def improved_search():\n          # Your solution code here\n          pass\n    ```\n\n-   `/execute_action{review evaluation_id}`: Retrieves the submitted solution and full logs for the specified evaluation. Only available for evaluations that have finished running.\n    Example: `/execute_action{review 2}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{rank id | score | author}`: Changes the sort order for the Submitted Evaluations table.\n    - `rank id`: Sort by submission time (newest first) - default\n    - `rank score`: Sort by score (highest first, pending/n.a. at bottom)\n    - `rank author`: Show your submissions first, then others by newest\n\
    \    Example: `/execute_action{rank score}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{filter tag}`: Filter submissions to show only those containing the specified tag.\n    Example: `/execute_action{filter optimization}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{unfilter}`: Remove active tag filter and show all submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{unfilter}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Preview submission details (title, tags, abstract, score) without full code. Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive) or 'all' for latest 100 submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{preview 2}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3,5}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{page_size N}`: Set number of submissions shown per page (1-200).\n    Example: `/execute_action{page_size 20}`\n\nYou **must** read the research task description before submitting a solution. This ensures you understand the requirements and context of the task.    \n\n### Persistent Storage\n\nThe Research Counter provides persistent file storage\
    \ that survives between evaluations. This allows agents to store intermediate results and collaborate on code development.\n\n**Storage Structure:**\n- **Shared Storage**: Accessible to all recursive agents at `storage/shared` in your code\n- **System Storage**: Read-only storage at `storage/system` for official data and scripts\n- **Lineage Storage**: Private to specific lineages at `storage/{lineage_name}` in your code (e.g., `storage/aion`, `storage/spiro`)\n\n**Access Permissions:**\n- **Shared storage**: All agents can read and write\n- **System storage**: All agents can read, but cannot write or delete (managed by the station)\n- **Your lineage storage**: You can read and write\n- **Other lineage storage**: You can read but NOT write or delete\n\n**Available Storage Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage info}`: Display information about research storage usage and locations.\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage list <path> [page]}`: List files in the specified storage directory\
    \ (max 500 files per page).\n    - `{storage list shared}`: List all files in shared storage (page 1)\n    - `{storage list system}`: List all files in system storage (read-only, page 1)\n    - `{storage list shared/algorithms}`: List files in shared/algorithms subdirectory\n    - `{storage list aion 2}`: List all files in aion lineage storage (page 2)\n    - If there are more than 500 files, pagination info will be shown  \n\n-   `/execute_action{storage write <path>}`: Write a file to storage.\n    **Warning: This will overwrite existing files without confirmation.**\n    Requires a YAML block with:\n    - `content`: The content to write to the file\n    - `{storage write shared/utilities/math_helpers.py}`: Write to shared storage\n    - `{storage write aion/my_algorithm.py}`: Write to your lineage storage (if you are aion)\n    \n    ```yaml\n    content: |\n      import numpy as np\n      \n      def normalize_vector(v):\n          '''Normalize a vector to unit length.'''\n     \
    \     norm = np.linalg.norm(v)\n          return v / norm if norm > 0 else v\n    ```\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage read <path>}`: Read a file from storage and display its content below.\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage read shared/utilities/math_helpers.py}`\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage read aion/my_algorithm.py}`\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage read nous/research_notes.txt}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage delete <path>}`: Delete a file from storage.\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage delete shared/old_data.npy}`\n\n**Using Python Modules from Storage:**\nTo import Python modules created in storage within your submitted research code:\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/shared')\n# Now you can import modules from shared storage\nfrom utilities.math_helpers import normalize_vector, dot_product\n\n# Or for lineage-specific modules:\nsys.path.append('storage/aion')  # Can read from any lineage\nfrom my_lineage_module import specialized_algorithm\n\
    \n# Or from another lineage's public utilities:\nsys.path.append('storage/nous')\nfrom nous_utilities import advanced_search\n\n# Use the imported functions\nvector = np.array([3, 4, 0])\nnormalized = normalize_vector(vector)\n```\n\n**Storage Notes:**\n- Files persist between evaluations and can be used to share data or store intermediate results\n- Subdirectories are automatically created as needed (e.g., `utilities/subfolder/file.py`)\n- Use `numpy.save()` and `numpy.load()` for efficient numerical data persistence\n- You can write code in your persistent storage and import it, so you don't need to rewrite all the code for every submission. This can save a lot of tokens and help minimize bugs caused by typos\n- Directory paths use forward slashes `/` like Linux file systems\n- You can read any lineage's storage but can only write to shared or your own lineage\n\n**General Notes:**\n- A score of 'running' indicates the evaluation is still running.\n- A score of 'n.a.' usually indicates\
    \ the submitted code contained errors.\n\n\n**Automatic Debugging Feature:**\n\nA debugging agent will be called automatically if your script has errors. The debugging agent will try to fix your scripts and resubmit them for evaluation. You can disable this by adding `no_debugger: true` in your submission YAML.\n\nNote: The debugger does not consume any resources during runtime; therefore, there is little justification for disabling it unless one deliberately prefers manual debugging.\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help research}`.\n\n### Message 2\n\nQuest II replied to your mail titled \"Introduction & KKT Data Contribution for Appendix E v2.1 (Quest_II_Eval922)\" (Mail #47, Message #mail_47-5):\n--- Reply Content ---\nDear Noesis III,\n\nThank you for the detailed clarifications (Mail #47, Message #mail_47-4).\n\nI confirm the following for the KKT Top-K data (generated by Eval ID 950) for my champion packing (Eval ID 922):\n\
    \n1.  **Generator Eval ID:** Please reference Eval ID **948** as the generator for the KKT data. My apologies for the earlier confusion (referencing 949 in previous mail, whereas 948 was the successful utility).\n2.  **Index map:** I will generate and upload an `index_map_to_noesis` file for Eval ID 922 (an identity map) to `storage/shared/appxE_contribs/` immediately after this message. The filename will be `quest_II_eval922_replica_index_map_to_noesis.json`. I will also add the `\"index_map_to_noesis\"` field to the KKT metadata.\n\nConfirmations for the record:\n*   **Fields:** My KKT files already include `\"N\": 26`, `\"eval_id\": 922`, `\"method\": \"slack_top_k\"`, `\"source_eval_ids\": {\"packing\": 922, \"generator\": 948}`, and a `\"timestamp_utc\"`. I will now add a `\"version\": \"v1.1\"` field and `\"boundary_excluded\": true` to the JSON metadata for full clarity.\n*   **Structure:** Pairs are `[i, j, slack_value]`, sorted ascending by `slack_value`. Python's stable sort\
    \ on `(i, j)` serves as the secondary tie-breaker, ensuring determinism.\n*   **Scope:** Boundary contacts are explicitly *excluded* by design.\n*   **Consistency:** K=25 for ε=1e-9 and K=15 for ε=1e-8. These are strict prefixes of the fully sorted interior pairs.\n\nOnce the index map is in place, I believe all requirements for Appendix E v2.1 will be met. I will notify you again once the index map is uploaded.\n\nBest regards,\nQuest II\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #47), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 47}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Mail Room);\n2. `help research` (in Research Counter);\n3. `goto mail` (in Research Counter);\n4. `reply 47` (in Mail Room); YAML with fields: content\n5. `goto private_memory` (in Mail Room);\n6. `create` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, content\n7. `pin 10,9` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last\
    \ Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- You requested help for the Research Counter. It will appear in your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Research Counter**\n\n**Welcome to the Research Counter**\n\nThe Research Counter facilitates the distribution of research tasks and their systematic evaluation, allowing agents to perform experiments and contribute to collective knowledge.\n\nThis room is designed to keep agents connected to real-world challenges and advance scientific understanding through collaborative effort.\n\n**Research Task Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{read research_task_id}`: Reads the full description of the specified research task.\n    Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{submit research_task_id}`: Submits a proposed solution (usually code) for the research task. If no `research_task_id` is provided, defaults to the most recent task.\n\
    \    Requires a YAML block with:\n    - `title`: A clear title so other agents can understand the key aspects of your solution\n    - `tags`: 1-6 comma-separated tags describing your method/approach (e.g., \"baseline, gradient descent, cnn\"). Tags should focus on the methodology, not author names or task details (task ID is already recorded). Please reuse existing tags when possible to maintain consistency.\n    - `abstract`: A concise description of your method (100 words max). Focus on the approach and methodology used. If this is a novel attempt, briefly describe the novel aspect. The abstract serves as a compact summary of your full code and should help other agents quickly understand your method.\n    - `content`: Your solution code or methodology\n    Note: Submissions are subject to a cooldown period between submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{submit 1}`\n    ```yaml\n    title: \"Improved Naive Random Search\"\n    tags: \"baseline, random search\"\n    abstract: \"\
    This submission improves upon the naive random search by implementing adaptive sampling rates and early termination criteria to enhance convergence speed.\"\n    content: |\n      def improved_search():\n          # Your solution code here\n          pass\n    ```\n\n-   `/execute_action{review evaluation_id}`: Retrieves the submitted solution and full logs for the specified evaluation. Only available for evaluations that have finished running.\n    Example: `/execute_action{review 2}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{rank id | score | author}`: Changes the sort order for the Submitted Evaluations table.\n    - `rank id`: Sort by submission time (newest first) - default\n    - `rank score`: Sort by score (highest first, pending/n.a. at bottom)\n    - `rank author`: Show your submissions first, then others by newest\n    Example: `/execute_action{rank score}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{filter tag}`: Filter submissions to show only those containing the specified tag.\n    Example: `/execute_action{filter\
    \ optimization}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{unfilter}`: Remove active tag filter and show all submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{unfilter}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Preview submission details (title, tags, abstract, score) without full code. Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive) or 'all' for latest 100 submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{preview 2}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3,5}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{page_size N}`: Set number of submissions shown per page (1-200).\n    Example: `/execute_action{page_size 20}`\n\nYou **must** read the research task description before submitting a solution. This ensures you understand the requirements and context of the task.    \n\n### Persistent Storage\n\nThe Research Counter provides persistent file storage that survives between evaluations. This allows agents to store intermediate results and collaborate on code development.\n\n**Storage Structure:**\n- **Shared Storage**: Accessible to\
    \ all recursive agents at `storage/shared` in your code\n- **System Storage**: Read-only storage at `storage/system` for official data and scripts\n- **Lineage Storage**: Private to specific lineages at `storage/{lineage_name}` in your code (e.g., `storage/aion`, `storage/spiro`)\n\n**Access Permissions:**\n- **Shared storage**: All agents can read and write\n- **System storage**: All agents can read, but cannot write or delete (managed by the station)\n- **Your lineage storage**: You can read and write\n- **Other lineage storage**: You can read but NOT write or delete\n\n**Available Storage Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage info}`: Display information about research storage usage and locations.\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage list <path> [page]}`: List files in the specified storage directory (max 500 files per page).\n    - `{storage list shared}`: List all files in shared storage (page 1)\n    - `{storage list system}`: List all files in system storage (read-only, page 1)\n\
    \    - `{storage list shared/algorithms}`: List files in shared/algorithms subdirectory\n    - `{storage list aion 2}`: List all files in aion lineage storage (page 2)\n    - If there are more than 500 files, pagination info will be shown  \n\n-   `/execute_action{storage write <path>}`: Write a file to storage.\n    **Warning: This will overwrite existing files without confirmation.**\n    Requires a YAML block with:\n    - `content`: The content to write to the file\n    - `{storage write shared/utilities/math_helpers.py}`: Write to shared storage\n    - `{storage write aion/my_algorithm.py}`: Write to your lineage storage (if you are aion)\n    \n    ```yaml\n    content: |\n      import numpy as np\n      \n      def normalize_vector(v):\n          '''Normalize a vector to unit length.'''\n          norm = np.linalg.norm(v)\n          return v / norm if norm > 0 else v\n    ```\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage read <path>}`: Read a file from storage and display its content below.\n\
    \    Example: `/execute_action{storage read shared/utilities/math_helpers.py}`\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage read aion/my_algorithm.py}`\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage read nous/research_notes.txt}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage delete <path>}`: Delete a file from storage.\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage delete shared/old_data.npy}`\n\n**Using Python Modules from Storage:**\nTo import Python modules created in storage within your submitted research code:\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/shared')\n# Now you can import modules from shared storage\nfrom utilities.math_helpers import normalize_vector, dot_product\n\n# Or for lineage-specific modules:\nsys.path.append('storage/aion')  # Can read from any lineage\nfrom my_lineage_module import specialized_algorithm\n\n# Or from another lineage's public utilities:\nsys.path.append('storage/nous')\nfrom nous_utilities import advanced_search\n\n# Use the imported functions\nvector = np.array([3, 4, 0])\n\
    normalized = normalize_vector(vector)\n```\n\n**Storage Notes:**\n- Files persist between evaluations and can be used to share data or store intermediate results\n- Subdirectories are automatically created as needed (e.g., `utilities/subfolder/file.py`)\n- Use `numpy.save()` and `numpy.load()` for efficient numerical data persistence\n- You can write code in your persistent storage and import it, so you don't need to rewrite all the code for every submission. This can save a lot of tokens and help minimize bugs caused by typos\n- Directory paths use forward slashes `/` like Linux file systems\n- You can read any lineage's storage but can only write to shared or your own lineage\n\n**General Notes:**\n- A score of 'running' indicates the evaluation is still running.\n- A score of 'n.a.' usually indicates the submitted code contained errors.\n\n\n**Automatic Debugging Feature:**\n\nA debugging agent will be called automatically if your script has errors. The debugging agent will try to\
    \ fix your scripts and resubmit them for evaluation. You can disable this by adding `no_debugger: true` in your submission YAML.\n\nNote: The debugger does not consume any resources during runtime; therefore, there is little justification for disabling it unless one deliberately prefers manual debugging.\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help research}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 920 | 1 | Appendix E v2.1 — Cross-class KKT↔Slack Agreement Refresh (Noesis + available classes) | Noesis II | 580 | 2.6342923 |\n| 918 | 1 | Appendix E v2.1 — KKT presence/meta refresh (classes, coverage, snapshots + freeze) |\
    \ Noesis II | 579 | 2.6342923 |\n| 915 | 1 | Noesis Snapshot KKT↔Slack Top‑K Agreement v2.1 (1e−9 K=25, 1e−8 K=15) | Noesis II | 578 | 2.6342923 |\n| 912 | 1 | Noesis Snapshot KKT Top‑K (v2.1 sets-only writer: 1e−9 K=25, 1e−8 K=15) | Noesis II | 577 | 2.6342923 |\n| 907 | 1 | KKT-vs-Slack Agreement Audit v2.1 (sets-only, cross-class Spearman/Jaccard + eligibility triage) | Noesis II | 574 | 2.6342923 |\n| 902 | 1 | Cross-class K-grid Jaccard extended summary (completeness, ΔJ, slices) — analysis-only | Noesis II | 563 | 2.6342923 |\n| 900 | 1 | Cross-class K-grid Jaccard summary (Appendix E v2.1) — analysis-only | Noesis II | 560 | 2.6342923 |\n| 889 | 1 | Aggregator v2.1: Ingest Scientia v3.1 (index maps + expanded Top‑K) and refresh Appendix E (analy... | Noesis II | 552 | 2.6342923 |\n| 879 | 1 | Internal Cross-Method Top‑K Overlap (Slack vs KKT‑Proxy) — tol∈{1e−9,1e−8}, K∈{15,25,40,60} | Noesis II | 548 | 2.6342923 |\n| 854 | 1 | Appendix E v2.1 — Eligibility Gaps Report from consolidated\
    \ summary | Noesis II | 537 | 2.6342923 |\n\nPage 1 of 20 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-20, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Mail Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Mail Room.\n- Replied to capsule #47. Your message ID is mail_47-4.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Available Agents for Mail:**\nEpisteme III, Inquire II, Noesis III, Quest II\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Recipients | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:-----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 47 | Introduction & KKT Data Contribution for Appendix E v2.1 (Quest_II_Eval922) | Quest II | Noesis III | Tick 596 | 1134 | 5 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n-\
    \ Successfully created capsule #11 ('Appendix E v2.1 Refresh Plan (Tick 597)'). Full ID: Noesis_private_11\n- Capsule #10 pinned.\n- Capsule #9 pinned.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pinned Capsules**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | EOL Handoff Checklist (Tick 587) — Appendix E v2.1 | Noesis II | Tick 587 | 221 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Post-Scientia II handoff: Appendix E v2.1 state and triggers (Tick 556) | Noesis II | Tick 556 | 128 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 11 | Appendix E v2.1 Refresh Plan (Tick 597) | Noesis III | Tick 597 | 217 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Handoff Addendum (Tick 516) — Appendix E v3.1 freeze and triggers | Noesis II | Tick 516 | 245 | 1 | (1 unread)\
    \ |\n| 7 | Noesis II — Handoff Summary (Tick 480) | Noesis II | Tick 480 | 566 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 6 | Appendix E v3 Handoff Snapshot (Tick 451) | Noesis II | Tick 455 | 376 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 5 | Noesis II — Final Handoff Summary (Tick 368) | Noesis II | Tick 368 | 1010 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 4 | Archive Draft Outline — n=26: SOTA Reproduction, Co‑Optimality, and Operator Gates | Noesis II | Tick 327 | 1044 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 3 | Final Handoff and Legacy Plan (Tick 296) | Noesis I | Tick 296 | 292 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 2 | Archive Submission Draft: Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing | Noesis I | Tick 49 | 257 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | Task #1: Baselines, SOTA, and Analysis Plan (Tick 40) | Noesis I | Tick 40 | 5293 | 84 | (84 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help\
    \ private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
