role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adaptive SA with pair/boundary/triple moves + greedy quenches (reproduction of best variant, 9‑min)\"\ntags: \"optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, pair-moves, boundary, triple, modular\"\nabstract: \"Reproduces the 2.574 best variant: adaptive SA over centers with weighted tight‑pair separation, boundary‑biased nudges, and triple neighbor proposals, interleaved with greedy coordinate quenches. Uses exact LP radii (HiGHS), multistart seeds from lineage module, 9‑minute budget, and final LP‑tighten.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, time, numpy as np\n  sys.path.append('storage/noesis')\n  from pack.lp_sa_core import (\n      lp_radii_for_centers, multistart_seeds, clamp_unit,\n      pair_slacks, boundary_slacks, coordinate_quench\n  )\n\n  EPS = 1e-12\n  IMPROVE_TOL = 1e-12\n\n  def sa_adaptive_triple(C0, time_stop, seed_offset=0,\n                         T0=7e-4, Tmin=1e-7, alpha=0.996,\n                         step0=0.04,\n\
    \                         pair_w=0.45, boundary_w=0.25, triple_w=0.30,\n                         trials_per_T=95, top_k_pairs=100):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(20251021 + seed_offset)\n      r, obj, ok = lp_radii_for_centers(C0)\n      if not ok:\n          return C0.copy(), r, obj\n      C = C0.copy()\n      # Initial greedy quench\n      C, r, obj = coordinate_quench(C, r, obj, base_step=0.03, loops=1, time_stop=time_stop)\n\n      T = T0\n      step = step0\n      best_C = C.copy(); best_r = r.copy(); best_obj = obj\n      stagn = 0\n\n      while T > Tmin and (not time_stop or time.time() < time_stop):\n          pairs, iu, dist = pair_slacks(C, r)\n          bmins, bdirs = boundary_slacks(C, r)\n          b_order = np.argsort(bmins)\n\n          # Weighted selection over tight pairs ~ 1/(slack + δ)\n          if len(pairs) > 0:\n              delta = 1e-9\n              sl = np.array([max(p[2], 0.0) for p in pairs], dtype=float)\n              w = 1.0 / (sl + delta)\n   \
    \           w = w / (np.sum(w) + 1e-18)\n              cumw = np.cumsum(w)\n\n          accepted = 0\n          for _ in range(trials_per_T):\n              if time_stop and time.time() >= time_stop: break\n              trial = C.copy()\n              u = rng.random()\n              if u < pair_w and len(pairs) > 0:\n                  # Tight-pair separation\n                  k = int(np.searchsorted(cumw, rng.random()))\n                  k = min(k, len(pairs)-1)\n                  i, j, _ = pairs[k]\n                  v = C[i] - C[j]\n                  d = np.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-18\n                  dir_vec = v / d\n                  trial[i] = clamp_unit(trial[i] + dir_vec * step)\n                  trial[j] = clamp_unit(trial[j] - dir_vec * step)\n\n              elif u < pair_w + boundary_w:\n                  # Boundary-limited inward nudge\n                  i = int(b_order[0])\n                  dir_vec = bdirs[i]\n                  trial[i] = clamp_unit(trial[i] + dir_vec\
    \ * step)\n\n              else:\n                  # Triple proposal: move smallest boundary‑limited circle away from two nearest neighbors\n                  # Build a simple two-neighbor away vector; if degenerate, fall back to random\n                  i = int(b_order[0]) if len(C) > 0 else 0\n                  diff = C - C[i]\n                  d = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff * diff, axis=1))\n                  order = np.argsort(d)\n                  nbrs = [j for j in order if j != i][:2]\n                  if len(nbrs) == 2 and d[nbrs[1]] > 1e-12:\n                      u1 = (C[i] - C[nbrs[0]]) / (np.linalg.norm(C[i] - C[nbrs[0]]) + 1e-18)\n                      u2 = (C[i] - C[nbrs[1]]) / (np.linalg.norm(C[i] - C[nbrs[1]]) + 1e-18)\n                      move = u1 + u2\n                      move /= (np.linalg.norm(move) + 1e-18)\n                      trial[i] = clamp_unit(trial[i] + move * step)\n                      # push neighbors slightly apart as well\n                      trial[nbrs[0]]\
    \ = clamp_unit(trial[nbrs[0]] - 0.5 * move * step)\n                      trial[nbrs[1]] = clamp_unit(trial[nbrs[1]] - 0.5 * move * step)\n                  else:\n                      # Random single or opposite pair\n                      if rng.random() < 0.5:\n                          i = int(rng.integers(0, len(C)))\n                          dvec = rng.normal(size=2); dvec /= (np.linalg.norm(dvec) + 1e-12)\n                          trial[i] = clamp_unit(trial[i] + dvec * step)\n                      else:\n                          i = int(rng.integers(0, len(C)))\n                          j = int(rng.integers(0, len(C)-1));  j = j if j < i else j + 1\n                          dvec = rng.normal(size=2); dvec /= (np.linalg.norm(dvec) + 1e-12)\n                          trial[i] = clamp_unit(trial[i] + dvec * step)\n                          trial[j] = clamp_unit(trial[j] - dvec * step)\n\n              r_try, obj_try, ok = lp_radii_for_centers(trial)\n              if not ok:\n\
    \                  continue\n              delta_obj = obj_try - obj\n              accept = (delta_obj > IMPROVE_TOL) or (rng.random() < np.exp(delta_obj / max(T, 1e-12)))\n              if accept:\n                  C, r, obj = trial, r_try, obj_try\n                  accepted += 1\n                  # Greedy coordinate quench after improvements (micro linesearch heuristic)\n                  if delta_obj > 0 and rng.random() < 0.3:\n                      C, r, obj = coordinate_quench(C, r, obj, base_step=max(step*0.7, 0.0015), loops=1, time_stop=time_stop)\n                  if obj > best_obj + IMPROVE_TOL:\n                      best_C, best_r, best_obj = C.copy(), r.copy(), obj\n\n          # Adaptive step by acceptance ratio\n          acc_ratio = accepted / max(trials_per_T, 1)\n          if acc_ratio < 0.10:\n              step *= 0.85\n          elif acc_ratio > 0.40:\n              step *= 1.08\n          step = float(np.clip(step, 0.0015, 0.08))\n\n          # Cooling and\
    \ occasional polish\n          old_best = best_obj\n          T *= alpha\n          if best_obj <= old_best + 1e-18:\n              stagn += 1\n          else:\n              stagn = 0\n          if stagn >= 6:\n              # Mild reheating and tiny jitter around best\n              T = min(T * 1.25, T0)\n              stagn = 0\n              jitter = 0.0025\n              best_C = np.clip(best_C + np.random.uniform(-jitter, jitter, size=best_C.shape), 1e-3, 1.0 - 1e-3)\n              r_b, obj_b, ok_b = lp_radii_for_centers(best_C)\n              if ok_b and obj_b > best_obj + IMPROVE_TOL:\n                  best_r, best_obj = r_b, obj_b\n\n          if not time_stop or time.time() < time_stop:\n              C, r, obj = coordinate_quench(C, r, obj, base_step=max(step*0.5, 0.0015), loops=1, time_stop=time_stop)\n\n      # Final tiny polish\n      if not time_stop or time.time() < time_stop:\n          best_C, best_r, best_obj = coordinate_quench(best_C, best_r, best_obj, base_step=0.002,\
    \ loops=1, time_stop=time_stop)\n      return best_C, best_r, best_obj\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      # 9-minute total budget\n      BUDGET_S = 540.0\n      t0 = time.time()\n      time_stop = t0 + BUDGET_S\n\n      # Multistart\n      seeds = multistart_seeds()\n\n      best_obj = -1.0\n      best_C = None\n      best_r = None\n\n      # Round-robin over seeds with different offsets to vary RNG\n      for si, C0 in enumerate(seeds):\n          if time.time() >= time_stop: break\n          C_sa, r_sa, obj_sa = sa_adaptive_triple(C0, time_stop=time_stop, seed_offset=si,\n                                                  T0=7e-4, Tmin=1e-7, alpha=0.996,\n                                                  step0=0.04, pair_w=0.45, boundary_w=0.25, triple_w=0.30,\n                                                  trials_per_T=95, top_k_pairs=100)\n          if obj_sa > best_obj + 1e-12:\n              best_obj = obj_sa\n              best_C = C_sa\n              best_r = r_sa\n\n   \
    \   # Final LP tighten for safety\n      r_final, obj_final, ok = lp_radii_for_centers(best_C)\n      if ok:\n          best_r = r_final\n      return best_C.astype(float), best_r.astype(float)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering experiment parameters**\n\nI’ve found that many experiments degrade with longer budgets, and the best performance comes from an 8-minute budget, specifically ID 1008. This used adaptive simulated annealing with various techniques. I wonder if tuning parameters like pair weights or acceptance ratios could help. It could be useful to exploit the best configuration from ID 1008 while incorporating small adjustments within a 12-minute budget. Restarting instead of a single long run seems wiser since extended budgets can lead to degradation.**Designing a submission strategy**\n\nI’m thinking about creating a submission that utilizes the best module code while having an orchestrator conduct multiple independent simulated annealing (SA) runs, ideally every 6 minutes. Since evaluation takes 20 minutes, I can budget 12 minutes for restarts and possibly a small refinement. Given that ID 1008 achieved results in 8 minutes, increasing the budget to 12 minutes through\
  \ restarts could work. Let’s consider running 6 trials in this duration and then incorporating a local coordinate-line search to enhance improvements efficiently.**Assessing proposal effectiveness**\n\nI've noticed that \"triple proposals\" didn't seem very helpful, despite the best performance from ID 1008, which did use them. Interestingly, when ID 1016 tried size-aware and micro-line approaches, the results dropped. \n\nThe best code from 1008, labeled \"Adaptive LP-tightened SA with weighted pair/boundary/triple proposals\", scored 2.5743. I could modularize this code to replicate its success or submit an improved version with minor tweaks. It might be worth tuning probabilities for pairs, boundaries, and triples, but the best path seems to involve reconstructing the 1008 methods for better results.**Considering code replication**\n\nI realize we already have \"lp_sa_core.py\" in storage, which includes the size-aware simulated annealing (SA) and dual refinement. It seems we could\
  \ extend this module by adding a function for \"sa_adaptive_triple\" to replicate code from ID 1008. However, if we can't locate that code, we might need to review ID 1008's code. The Research Counter supports reviewing evaluations, so I'm thinking I should review 1008 to access the full code and logs. This could help in replicating the function accurately while being cautious about token limits.**Proposing a new submission**\n\nI'm considering an alternative approach where I can modify the existing module to include triple moves and boundary slack, using the size-aware SA that's already developed. Although the earlier attempt with ID 1016 performed poorly, we did achieve a score of 2.542 with dual-guided methods.\n\nI think it would be wise to submit a code implementation that executes the \"sa_pair_boundary\" function with adjusted probabilities. Also, reviewing ID 1008 shows effective adaptations in its method, so I plan to replicate and run it with different random seeds and tighter\
  \ parameters to potentially enhance results. I also want to ensure we avoid code duplication and streamline with existing module functions. Proceeding to finalize and submit!"
