tick: 636
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write noesis/pack/results_summary.json}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  {\n    \"task\": \"n=26 circle packing\",\n    \"evaluations\": [\n      {\"id\": 971,  \"title\": \"Greedy + local coord search\",                                            \"score\": 1.4878001},\n      {\"id\": 974,  \"title\": \"Equal-grid baseline (max equal radius)\",                                 \"score\": 1.8571428},\n      {\"id\": 977,  \"title\": \"SLSQP from equal-grid init\",                                             \"score\": 1.8571428},\n      {\"id\": 980,  \"title\": \"Bilevel LP radii + coordinate search\",                                   \"score\": 2.4385658},\n      {\"id\": 982,  \"title\": \"Bilevel LP + annealed multistart coord/random\",                          \"score\": 2.4904137},\n      {\"id\": 985,  \"title\": \"Bilevel LP + tight-pair separation\",                                     \"score\": 2.4852655},\n      {\"id\"\
    : 995,  \"title\": \"Time‑budgeted LP‑tightened center search\",                               \"score\": 2.4920156},\n      {\"id\": 998,  \"title\": \"LP‑tightened global‑hole recentering (10m)\",                             \"score\": 2.4592625},\n      {\"id\": 1000, \"title\": \"LP‑tightened SA + greedy quenches (4m)\",                                 \"score\": 2.5342577},\n      {\"id\": 1003, \"title\": \"SA with pair/boundary bias (10m)\",                                       \"score\": 2.5299016},\n      {\"id\": 1008, \"title\": \"Adaptive SA (pair+boundary+triple) + quenches (8m)\",                     \"score\": 2.5742849},\n      {\"id\": 1012, \"title\": \"SA + SPSA fine‑tune (8m)\",                                               \"score\": 2.5097131},\n      {\"id\": 1016, \"title\": \"Size‑aware SA long budget (12m)\",                                        \"score\": 2.3601148},\n      {\"id\": 1019, \"title\": \"Modular size‑aware SA (12m)\",                      \
    \                      \"score\": 2.4802888},\n      {\"id\": 1023, \"title\": \"SA warm start (5m) + dual‑guided ascent (5m)\",                           \"score\": 2.5421034},\n      {\"id\": 1026, \"title\": \"Modular size‑aware SA (pair_exp=1.8, 10m)\",                              \"score\": 2.4375460},\n      {\"id\": 1029, \"title\": \"Ensemble short SA restarts + dual refine (12m)\",                         \"score\": 2.4745097},\n      {\"id\": 1031, \"title\": \"Reproduction: SA triple variant (9m)\",                                   \"score\": 2.5015677},\n      {\"id\": 1034, \"title\": \"Best SA triple variant (8m) re‑run\",                                     \"score\": 2.4527253},\n      {\"id\": 1036, \"title\": \"Block Pair Line‑Search (deterministic, LP‑in‑the‑loop)\",                 \"score\": 2.3067225},\n      {\"id\": 1039, \"title\": \"Compact SA+Quench (4.5m) + acceptance‑ratio step control\",               \"score\": 2.5406351}\n    ],\n    \"best\": {\"id\"\
    : 1008, \"score\": 2.5742849},\n    \"sota_reference\": 2.6342924\n  }\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft — LP-in-the-loop center search for n=26: methods, ablations, and results\"\ntags: [\"n26\", \"draft\", \"lp\", \"sa\", \"ablations\"]\ncontent: |\n  Abstract\n  We study LP-in-the-loop center search for 26-circle packing in the unit square, where an exact LP (HiGHS) computes optimal radii for fixed centers and an outer optimizer moves centers. Compact simulated annealing (SA) with weighted tight-pair separation, boundary nudges, occasional triple-neighbor proposals, and frequent greedy coordinate quenches achieves 2.5743 (Eval 1008). Longer budgets tend to drift; short runs (4–8 min) with acceptance-ratio control and mild reheating are more reliable.\n\n  Method\n  - Inner solver: LP on r∈R^26, maximize 1ᵀr s.t. r≥0, r≤ub(center), and r_i+r_j≤||c_i−c_j||−ε.\n  - Proposals:\n    * Tight-pair separation: sample pairs ∝ 1/(slack+δ),\
    \ move along ± separation.\n    * Boundary nudge: pick most boundary-limited circle, step inward.\n    * Triple move (modest weight): move the smallest boundary-limited circle away from its two nearest neighbors; push neighbors slightly apart.\n  - Greedy coordinate quench: per circle, probe 8 directions with a small step and commit if LP objective improves.\n  - Schedules: T0≈6–8e−4, Tmin=1e−7, α≈0.996, trials_per_T≈100, step≈0.04 with acceptance‑ratio control (target 0.25–0.35).\n  - Reheating: after ~6 stagnant levels, reheat temperature and jitter the best centers slightly.\n  - Multistart seeds: grid + jitters + two hex-like + jittered-hex (6 total).\n  - Finalization: LP tighten radii on the best centers.\n\n  Ablations (observed)\n  - Coord-only: fast but stuck (≤2.44).\n  - Pair-only: improves but stalls without quenches (≈2.49).\n  - +Triple (modest weight): occasional gains; too high weight can drift.\n  - Size-aware steps: mixed impact; can help early but increased drift long-run.\n\
    \  - SPSA fine-tune: expensive, no net gain over compact SA in our trials.\n  - Dual-guided ascent (HiGHS marginals): reliable local polish (≈+0.01 over SA warm start).\n\n  Results (key)\n  - 2.5743 (Eval 1008): 8-min SA+quench (pair/boundary/triple).\n  - 2.5421 (Eval 1023): 5-min SA warm start + 5-min dual trust-region ascent.\n  - 2.5406 (Eval 1039): compact SA+quench (4.5 min).\n  (See storage/noesis/pack/results_summary.json for full table; SOTA ref 2.6343.)\n\n  Repro\n  - Frozen recipe: storage/noesis/pack/recipe_2_57_freeze.json\n  - Modular core: storage/noesis/pack/lp_sa_core.py\n  - Quick script: storage/noesis/pack/README.md (8-min run + LP tighten)\n\n  Discussion\n  We hypothesize that compact runs with frequent quenches better preserve pair structure by avoiding slow diffusion of centers under noisy updates. Pair‑weighted sampling and acceptance‑ratio step control stabilize progress. Short SA + brief dual ascent refine is promising when budgets are tight.\n\n  Next\n\
    \  - Upon maturity: prepare Archive note with full results table and code pointers.\n  - If resuming experiments: 4–5 min SA + 3–5 min dual polish; or two 4‑min SA restarts, pick best, 2‑min dual.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning research documentation**


  I could prepare a minimal script for storage to reproduce the best run. I''ve already written a README with reproduction steps and recipe JSON, which is good. I want to quickly attempt a top-performing variant to see if we can match or surpass the results. However, I need to be careful with token usage, so no more experiments for now. I''ll create a compact "analysis note" in private memory for future reference and start drafting a new capsule with key sections.


  **Organizing evaluation data**


  I want to keep things concise to save tokens, but still informative. I could write a results.csv file with evaluation IDs and scores for future reference since I already know some eval IDs from earlier system messages. I''ll create a small JSON mapping in storage, which is manageable within our token budget. It''ll be helpful to note that our score of 2.5743 isn''t state-of-the-art compared to 2.6343. Lastly, I''ll draft the capsule and make a note in Private Memory to revisit later.


  **Organizing research actions**


  I''m ready to proceed with a few actions in Private Memory. First, I’ll write to storage, creating the "results_summary.json" file that maps IDs, titles, and scores. I have the exact scores ready from previous messages, and I want to make sure I include most of them. After that, I’ll head back to Private Memory to draft a capsule titled, "Draft: LP-in-the-loop center search (n=26)," including sections for methods and ablations while being mindful of token use.


  **Drafting pseudocode**


  I should consider drafting a pseudocode snippet for the SA loop to include in the capsule. This will help clarify the thought process and provide a clearer structure for anyone using the capsule later. I need to make sure it''s straightforward and accurately reflects the steps in the loop while remaining concise. Let''s move forward with that! I''ll organize my thoughts so that the pseudocode blends well with the overall content.'
