- text: "/execute_action{review 1069}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Compact SA‑LP 8‑minute reproduction (hex seed, deterministic)\"\ntags: \"optimization, simulated annealing, lp, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Reproduces the stronger compact SA‑LP recipe: SA on centers with acceptance‑ratio control, periodic greedy quenches, and sparse exact LP solves; 480s budget from hex seed with fixed RNG for determinism.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np, sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noesis/pack')\n  from sa_lp import run_sa\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      centers, radii = run_sa(budget_sec=480.0, seed=20251021)\n      # Validity checks\n      assert centers.shape == (26,2)\n      assert radii.shape == (26,)\n      assert np.all(radii > 0)\n      x, y = centers[:,0], centers[:,1]\n      b = np.minimum.reduce([x, 1-x, y, 1-y])\n      assert np.all(radii <= b + 1e-9)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**Crafting a research plan**\n\nI need to put together a research plan for the user at the Research Counter. They've been working on aggregator tasks and now on circle packing methods. The history shows prior ticks had significant SA progress at 2.574 value, but after pruning, our current SA values are slightly lower. We should continue running experiments and plan to write to the Archive with our analysis soon. It’s important to stick to the environment’s constraints and consider better initialization seeds.\n\n**Considering potential improvements**\n\nI've identified that the known state-of-the-art (SOTA) involves nudging contact pairs, based on previous works. Although we can't import SOTA packings or lineage code, I can use some concept-level ideas like \"nudging pairs and SLSQP refinement.\" I want to stick to our SA-LP methodology and optimize time distribution. It seems the best results came from an 8-minute SA schedule, so I propose a consistent schedule: maybe\
  \ 3 runs at 240 seconds each with additional polish time. We can also explore step size adaptation and incorporate boundary-anchored expansions. Oh, and the LP solving is quite heavy!\n\n**Optimizing pair moves**\n\nI'm thinking about optimizing the solving process. I could solve seldom, maybe every 40 accepts and after some quenching, which could speed things up, although the time currently seems fine.\n\nOne idea is to use \"contact graph aware pair moves\" by computing slacks from current radii to identify tight pairs. Then, I can push along the gradient to optimize distances. Additionally, using \"block relaxations\" for line search can help adjust center pair positions effectively.\n\nI also want to ensure stable randomness seeds and save results for reproducibility while managing code imports from our own storage.\n\n**Proposing new module for SA-LP**\n\nI’m thinking about proposing an additional module named 'sa_lp_contacts.py' for a new submission called \"Contact-guided SA-LP\
  \ (8 min, top-K slack).\" Given the last \"Dual guided\" variant didn't perform well, it seems our dual-coded approach may have mismanaged center movements. \n\nI want to rely on proven heuristics like acceptance ratio control, greedy quenches, and minimal heavy LP to aim for a final score over 2.55. I’ll replicate earlier successful parameters with fixed values for the new submission. Plus, we might consider a gradient-free approach for refinement, though it could be too heavy for 20 minutes.\n\n**Planning multiple experiments**\n\nI'm focusing on performing several experiments to optimize our results. First, I'm planning to re-run the known best recipe in 8 minutes. Then, I want to execute a 3-run session at 240 seconds plus 180 seconds for polishing, while monitoring a current run (1069) in progress.\n\nThere are several proposed actions: submit the \"Compact SA 8-min reproduction\" using adjusted parameters, review run 1069, and consider \"hotstarting\" centers from evaluation 1062.\
  \ I should also work on minimal code submissions for efficiency. I plan to execute these actions shortly."
