parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write noesis/pack/active_quench.py}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np, time\n  from math import sqrt\n  # Reuse LP/hex utilities from params module to stay consistent\n  from sa_lp_exact_params import solve_radii_lp, hex_centers_26, clamp01\n\n  def pair_slacks(centers, r):\n      # Compute pairwise distances and slacks d_ij - (r_i + r_j)\n      diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n      D = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=-1))\n      slacks = D - (r[:, None] + r[None, :])\n      return D, slacks\n\n  def boundary_slacks(centers, r):\n      x = centers[:, 0]; y = centers[:, 1]\n      b = np.minimum.reduce([x, 1.0 - x, y, 1.0 - y])\n      return b - r  # >= 0 ideally\n\n  def nearest_wall_normal(c):\n      x, y = c\n      dists = np.array([x, 1-x, y, 1-y], dtype=float)\n      k = int(np.argmin(dists))\n      if k == 0:  return np.array([+1.0, 0.0])\n      if k == 1:  return np.array([-1.0, 0.0])\n      if\
    \ k == 2:  return np.array([0.0, +1.0])\n      return np.array([0.0, -1.0])\n\n  def active_pair_quench(centers, budget_sec=540.0, seed=123, k_pairs=20,\n                         alpha0=3e-3, backtrack=0.5, alpha_min=1e-6,\n                         boundary_push=0.4):\n      \"\"\"\n      Greedy constraint-aware polish:\n      - Identify top-k tight pairs and tight boundary indices using current exact LP radii.\n      - For each tight pair (i,j): push i and j apart along the connecting line with backtracking line search.\n      - For tight boundary: push inward along nearest wall normal (scaled by alpha).\n      \"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      start = time.time()\n      centers = centers.copy()\n      r, S, _ = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n      best_C, best_r, best_S = centers.copy(), r.copy(), S\n\n      while True:\n          if time.time() - start > budget_sec:\n              break\n\n          # Compute tight sets\n          D, sl = pair_slacks(centers, r)\n \
    \         np.fill_diagonal(sl, np.inf)\n          # top-k smallest positive slacks\n          idx = np.unravel_index(np.argsort(sl, axis=None)[:k_pairs], sl.shape)\n          pairs = list(zip(idx[0], idx[1]))\n          bsl = boundary_slacks(centers, r)\n          tight_bound_ids = list(np.argsort(bsl)[:min(6, len(bsl))])\n\n          improved = False\n          # Pair pushes\n          for (i, j) in pairs:\n              if i == j: continue\n              v = centers[i] - centers[j]\n              norm = np.linalg.norm(v)\n              if norm < 1e-12:\n                  v = rng.normal(0.0, 1.0, size=2)\n                  norm = np.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-12\n              u = v / norm\n              alpha = alpha0\n              # backtracking line search\n              while alpha >= alpha_min:\n                  C2 = centers.copy()\n                  C2[i] = C2[i] + alpha * u\n                  C2[j] = C2[j] - alpha * u\n                  C2 = clamp01(C2)\n                  r2, S2,\
    \ _ = solve_radii_lp(C2)\n                  if S2 > S + 1e-12:\n                      centers, r, S = C2, r2, S2\n                      if S2 > best_S:\n                          best_C, best_r, best_S = C2.copy(), r2.copy(), S2\n                      improved = True\n                      break\n                  alpha *= backtrack\n              if time.time() - start > budget_sec:\n                  break\n          if time.time() - start > budget_sec:\n              break\n\n          # Boundary pushes (gentle)\n          for i in tight_bound_ids:\n              nrm = nearest_wall_normal(centers[i])\n              alpha = boundary_push * alpha0\n              while alpha >= alpha_min:\n                  C2 = centers.copy()\n                  C2[i] = C2[i] + alpha * nrm\n                  C2 = clamp01(C2)\n                  r2, S2, _ = solve_radii_lp(C2)\n                  if S2 > S + 1e-12:\n                      centers, r, S = C2, r2, S2\n                      if S2 > best_S:\n\
    \                          best_C, best_r, best_S = C2.copy(), r2.copy(), S2\n                      improved = True\n                      break\n                  alpha *= backtrack\n              if time.time() - start > budget_sec:\n                  break\n\n          # Small random jitter if no improvement in a full round\n          if not improved:\n              i = int(rng.integers(0, len(centers)))\n              C2 = centers.copy()\n              C2[i] = C2[i] + rng.normal(0.0, alpha0 * 0.25, size=2)\n              C2 = clamp01(C2)\n              r2, S2, _ = solve_radii_lp(C2)\n              if S2 > S:\n                  centers, r, S = C2, r2, S2\n                  if S2 > best_S:\n                      best_C, best_r, best_S = C2.copy(), r2.copy(), S2\n\n      return best_C, best_r, best_S\n\n  def run_sa_then_quench(sa_fn, sa_budget=360.0, quench_budget=540.0, seed=20251021):\n      # Seed via exact-accept SA-like function passed in (signature: returns centers, radii, score)\n\
    \      C0 = hex_centers_26()\n      # run seeding SA\n      centers_sa, radii_sa, score_sa = sa_fn(budget_sec=sa_budget, seed=seed)\n      # run active quench\n      best_C, best_r, best_S = active_pair_quench(centers_sa, budget_sec=quench_budget, seed=seed+1)\n      return best_C, best_r, best_S\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Active‑pair backtracking quench seeded by exact SA (6m + 9m)\"\ntags: \"optimization, lp, quench, line search, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Seeds with exact‑accept SA (~6 min), then runs a constraint‑aware greedy quench (~9 min) that backtracks along tight pair/boundary directions with exact LP evaluation. Deterministic seeds.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np, sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noesis/pack')\n  import sa_lp_exact_params as P\n  import active_quench as AQ\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      # Seed with exact SA for 6 minutes, then active‑pair quench for 9 minutes\n      centers, radii, score = AQ.run_sa_then_quench(P.exact_sa_params,\
    \ sa_budget=360.0, quench_budget=540.0, seed=20251021)\n      # Validity checks\n      assert centers.shape == (26,2)\n      assert radii.shape == (26,)\n      assert np.all(radii > 0)\n      x, y = centers[:,0], centers[:,1]\n      b = np.minimum.reduce([x, 1-x, y, 1-y])\n      assert np.all(radii <= b + 1e-9)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning code implementation**\n\nI'm considering how we can implement code modules in storage and submit evaluation experiments. I think we need to take steps to write a new module, submit evaluations, and update the archive with those results afterward. \n\nLet's focus on writing a storage module for \"active_pair_line_search.py\" and submitting a run combination that starts with an exact SA run for 6 minutes. It’s crucial to follow the rules about loading our own lineage storage while keeping research code compliance in mind.\n\n**Clarifying lineage storage rules**\n\nI remember the instructions: I can read any lineage's storage but can only write to shared or my own lineage. There’s a clear distinction; I can't write or delete from other lineage storage, which I need to keep in mind. The \"General Notes\" mention that loading configurations from other lineages isn’t allowed and could lead to session termination. However, I can load my own lineage storage. So, I can\
  \ use my freeze npz file to coordinate the process and safely rerun SA for 6 minutes.\n\n**Developing implementation strategy**\n\nI’m planning to write a module that computes an active set of centers. The goal is to find pairs where the sum of their radii is close to the distance minus a small threshold, as well as identifying boundary active indices. I might want to increase slack for the top-K pairs along their connecting vector while ensuring boundaries aren’t violated.\n\nFor this, we'll also need to perform a backtracking line search to improve the LP objective. Finally, I’ll propose focusing on the top active constraints by either dual weight or closeness, using \"tightness\" metrics for adjustment.\n\n**Setting up LP evaluation process**\n\nI’m evaluating the LP, and if it improves, I’ll accept the changes; if not, I’ll reduce alpha by a factor of 0.5 and try again. I’ll repeat this for several iterations.\n\nWe’re planning to run this for a time budget, like 600 seconds, with\
  \ iterative line search loops. The evaluation function can run for up to 1200 seconds, but I need to keep everything within a 20-minute limit. I’ll implement this in the new module \"active_quench.py,\" starting with centers initialized from hex or possibly an SA seed.\n\n**Crafting active quench evaluation**\n\nI’m considering reading freeze centers from a file, but using \"np.load\" is only allowed for my own lineage, so it’s simpler to run the SA directly. To keep code concise, I might implement everything inline, but since the user wants actions, I can create storage. I’ll also update the archive with evaluations 1084 and 1089.\n\nNext, I plan to write the \"active_quench.py\" module, submit the evaluation for \"Constraint-aware active-pair quench,\" and set a run time of around 10 minutes. I'll optimize my approach by trying to replicate previous results, knowing that replication might vary due to the compute environment. After that, I’ll implement the main logic for improvement steps\
  \ while ensuring the code meets all constraints."
