tick: 661
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Exact‑accept SA 9m + 3m active‑pair backtracking quench (tuned)\"\ntags: \"optimization, simulated annealing, linear programming, quench, exact\"\nabstract: \"Runs exact‑accept SA for 540s using the strong baseline settings, then applies a 180s constraint‑aware backtracking quench along tight pair/boundary directions (k_pairs=30, alpha0=2e‑3). Deterministic seeds.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np, sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noesis/pack')\n  import sa_lp_exact_params as P\n  import active_quench as AQ\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      # Phase 1: Exact-accept SA baseline for 9 minutes\n      centers_sa, radii_sa, score_sa = P.exact_sa_params(\n          budget_sec=540.0,\n          seed=20251021,\n          step0=0.035,\n          ar_lo=0.15, ar_hi=0.30,\n          quench_interval=12.0, quench_tries=60,\n          margins=(0.04, 0.06),\n          two_phase=True,\n          step_minmax_phase1=(0.005, 0.08),\n          step_minmax_phase2=(0.004,\
    \ 0.04),\n      )\n\n      # Phase 2: Active-pair backtracking quench for 3 minutes (tuned)\n      best_C, best_r, best_S = AQ.active_pair_quench(\n          centers_sa,\n          budget_sec=180.0,\n          seed=20251031,\n          k_pairs=30,\n          alpha0=2e-3,\n          backtrack=0.5,\n          alpha_min=1e-6,\n          boundary_push=0.4\n      )\n\n      # Validity checks\n      assert best_C.shape == (26,2)\n      assert best_r.shape == (26,)\n      assert np.all(best_r > 0)\n      x, y = best_C[:,0], best_C[:,1]\n      b = np.minimum.reduce([x, 1-x, y, 1-y])\n      assert np.all(best_r <= b + 1e-9)\n\n      return best_C, best_r\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing evaluation methods**\n\nI'm considering the objective being piecewise-linear in radii. I note that deriving the gradient via LP duals isn't readily codable right now. Implementing a numerical gradient using finite differences could be quite heavy. With a 20-minute evaluation budget, it seems we have to maximize our coding time.\n\nI’ll focus on achieving a score around 2.57 by sticking to known parameters, using seeds that previously produced similar results, and testing for reproducibility. However, I question if replicating results will actually be beneficial since scores on the scoreboard matter.\n\n**Exploring evaluation strategies**\n\nI'm considering proposing a \"best-of-3 exact SA\" approach for 8 minutes each to see if we can exceed a score of 2.5669. However, previous runs only reached 2.5409 with shorter budgets. Perhaps longer per-run budgets like 3x300 seconds (5 minutes each) could yield better results, fitting within our 20-minute cap. I’m contemplating\
  \ the idea of refining the acceptance ratio or adjusting the T0 factor. Additionally, I think we should document our findings in a Public Memory update, even though the user hasn’t requested it.\n\n**Planning next steps**\n\nI think the best move is to replicate the best exact SA using the same parameters for 9 minutes, adding a finishing quench for 3 more minutes, which keeps us within a 12-minute total. The previous 12-minute two-phase run reached 2.5607, while the 9-minute run hit 2.5669. Adding quench could help, so I’ll code a new submission using the defined budgets. After the runs, I plan to log the results in Archive capsule #28, waiting until results are in to respond. Checking the storage list of runs might not be necessary right now.\n\n**Submitting evaluation strategies**\n\nFor this round, I can submit an evaluation titled \"Exact SA 540s + 180s active quench.\" However, I noticed that a previous two-phase schedule with 12 minutes resulted in a score of slightly less than\
  \ 2.5607, so I have to be cautious about the impact of 9+3. It might be worth a shot, though! \n\nI’m proposing \"Exact SA 9m + active-pair quench 3m.\" While attempting to run \"Exact SA for two seeds\" could also work, our time constraints suggest keeping it simple. So, I'll stick to a one-run combination for now.\n\n**Implementing the next steps**\n\nI'll update the Public Memory with results after completing the evaluation. First, I need to implement a wrapper function to use our modules effectively. I’ll create a script that references `active_quench.run_sa_then_quench` using budgets of 540 and 180. Although the default \"k_pairs\" is 20, I can adjust it to 30 for my specific needs. \n\nI'll submit the evaluation with the planned parameters and ensure the code is concise, minimizing any unnecessary narrative. I’ll proceed with the actions, ready to submit my updates!"
