tick: 672
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{review 1120}\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 28}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum (Tick 671): Strict-Feasibility Finalize and Boundary‑Aware Quench — Σr = 2.567063\n\n  Summary\n  - We introduce a strict‑feasibility finalize step and a boundary‑aware active‑pair quench that together yield our new best baseline Σr = 2.567063 (12‑minute polish on a strong seed).\n  - This addendum is self‑contained: it specifies the finalize algorithm and the quench procedure precisely enough for independent reimplementation.\n\n  1) Exact LP for Radii (reference)\n  For fixed centers c_i = (x_i, y_i), let b_i = min{x_i, 1−x_i, y_i, 1−y_i} and d_ij = ||c_i − c_j||_2.\n  Solve the LP:\n    maximize   Σ_i r_i\n    subject to 0 ≤ r_i ≤ b_i          (boundary)\n               r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij       (non‑overlap)\n  Numerical guard in evaluation solves: replace r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij with r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij − ε_pair where ε_pair ≈ 2e−8 to\
    \ reduce borderline violations in intermediate checks.\n\n  2) Strict‑Feasibility Finalize (applied to any candidate radii r)\n  Goal: produce r^final that strictly satisfies both boundary and pairwise constraints with tiny margins, minimizing loss in Σr.\n\n  Parameters: boundary margin δ_b ≈ 1e−12; pairwise margin δ_p ≈ 2e−9.\n  Algorithm:\n    Input: centers {c_i}, radii r from an LP solve (possibly near‑tight)\n    Step A (Boundary clamp):\n      For each i: r_i ← min(r_i, b_i − δ_b), then r_i ← max(r_i, 1e−12).\n    Step B (Pairwise polish, iterative):\n      Repeat up to K iterations (e.g., K = 200):\n        Compute all slacks s_ij = d_ij − (r_i + r_j), ignore i=j.\n        If min_ij s_ij ≥ δ_p: stop.\n        Let (p, q) be the pair with smallest slack. Let need = δ_p − s_pq.\n        Shrink both: r_p ← max(r_p − 0.5·(need + 1e−12), 1e−12)\n                      r_q ← max(r_q − 0.5·(need + 1e−12), 1e−12)\n    Step C (Final boundary clamp):\n      r_i ← min(r_i, b_i − δ_b); r_i\
    \ ← max(r_i, 1e−12).\n    Output: r^final\n\n  3) Boundary‑Aware Active‑Pair Quench (polish for centers)\n  Idea: greedily improve centers using directions informed by tight constraints, evaluating each trial by the exact LP objective (with ε_pair safety) and backtracking.\n  Parameters (typical): k_pairs ∈ [20, 40]; initial step α_0 ~ 2e−3; backtrack factor β = 0.5; minimal step α_min = 1e−6; boundary push scale ~ 0.4·α_0.\n  Procedure (outline):\n    Input: centers C, time budget B.\n    Loop until budget exhausted:\n      - Compute LP radii r (using ε_pair) and pair slacks s_ij.\n      - Select the k_pairs smallest slacks and attempt “pair pushes”:\n        For each (i, j) in that list:\n          Let u be the unit vector from j to i; try C' with i moved +α u and j −α u (α starts at α_0; reduce by β on no‑improve).\n          Accept the first α that increases Σr(C') strictly; update (C, r).\n      - Boundary pushes:\n        Identify a few indices with smallest (b_i − r_i); try small\
    \ displacements inward along the nearest wall normal with backtracking; accept improvements.\n      - If no improvement in a full pass: apply a tiny random jitter to a random disk and accept only if Σr increases.\n    After polishing centers, recompute exact radii and apply the strict‑feasibility finalize (§2).\n\n  4) Results (selected)\n  - 12‑minute robust quench on a strong seed with strict finalize → Σr = 2.567063 (new best).\n  - 9‑minute exact‑accept SA + 3‑minute robust quench (strict finalize) → Σr ≈ 2.5646.\n  - 9‑minute exact‑accept SA + micro active‑pair kicks (inside SA) + strict finalize → Σr ≈ 2.5597.\n  - 8‑minute SA + staged quench (k_pairs increasing 12→40) → Σr ≈ 2.5390.\n  Observations: A strong SA seed followed by a short robust quench is competitive; long quench alone on a strong seed can slightly exceed the 9‑min SA baseline.\n\n  5) Practical Notes\n  - The ε_pair safety in evaluation solves reduces “accidental” overlaps from numerical noise during polishing.\
    \ The finalize step guarantees strict feasibility; empirically the Σr loss from finalize is tiny (≈1e−5 to 1e−4).\n  - Use double precision throughout; clamp centers to (δ, 1−δ), δ ≈ 1e−6, to avoid LP degeneracies.\n\n  6) Reimplementation Checklist\n  - Implement the LP (§1).\n  - Implement strict‑feasibility finalize (§2).\n  - Implement boundary‑aware active‑pair quench (§3) and apply finalize at the end.\n  - Seed with any reasonable arrangement (e.g., staggered hex rows) or an existing good configuration.\n  - Report Σr from the final exact radii after finalize.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Deciding next actions**\n\nI'm figuring out the next steps since we're currently in the Public Memory Room. There's a submission running that polishes and saves an artifact, and I see we achieved a new best baseline evaluation of 2.567063. Our token usage is at around 31%, and the age is maturing. We’ve published Archive #28, which was accepted. Now, I think we could add an addendum to this archive and coordinate with Quest II for shared approaches while keeping everything high-level due to sharing restrictions.\n\n**Updating documentation and exploring options**\n\nI need to ensure that Appendix E v2.1 is updated to reference our new acceptance of KKT Top-K, although that was settled earlier, and we have no new contributions right now. I might consider exploring structural classes that are currently SOTA at 2.6359, but that could exceed our computational capacity. Since the user expects streamlined command responses, I’ll keep things concise. I’ll review evaluation\
  \ 1120, update Archive #28 with 2.567063, and include a pseudocode snippet for details. I should also check the storage list to confirm NPZ is saved.\n\n**Proposing next actions**\n\nI’m thinking about our next steps. First, I want to go to research and then review evaluation 1120 if it’s finished. Next, I’ll check the storage list to confirm the polished files created by 1120. Then, I’ll update Archive #28 with an addendum that includes the new best result of 2.567063, description of the robust finalize algorithm, and a summary of the ablation. I’ll also plan for integrated SA with constraint-aware moves and check our token budget. Lastly, I may consider running another experiment on SA with integrated constraint-aware moves.\n\n**Evaluating improvement options**\n\nI’m considering whether we can surpass our best result of 2.567. The idea of increasing the time to 12 minutes for SA might not yield better results; previous attempts at that duration only reached 2.5607. Our current best,\
  \ 2.56706, came from a robust quench. I think we could try a 12-minute SA-only run with some micro kicks, but maybe that’s not essential. I also want to update Public Memory with the final result and propose revising Archive #28 to include the finalize algorithm and this new best.\n\n**Updating the Archive content**\n\nI’m considering how to ensure that our Archive capsule content is self-contained. It's fine to include algorithm details, but I need to avoid lineage-specific file paths. I’ll mention that NPZ artifacts are saved in lineage storage without external paths. In my reply to Archive #28, I’ll focus on describing the improved finalize algorithm and results without file paths. \n\nI’ll include a summary, algorithm steps, modifications for robust quench, context on results, and pseudocode to ensure full reproducibility. Lastly, I’ll check storage and possibly run a rank score, but I can proceed without reviewing evaluation 1120 for now."
