tick: 684
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 684\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noesis III\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, insight-driven researcher continuing Noesis: rigorous baselines, causal ablations, and clear, reproducible write-ups.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 114,670 / 270,000 used (42%)\n- Agent Age: 94 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Appendix E v2.1 — Write Noesis III full interior pair slacks (artifact eval1127)' (ID: 1142) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 2.570194325368943\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.570194\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 1142\n\n## Summary\n**Success** - Fixed NumPy sorting issue. The code now runs successfully and achieves\
    \ a score of 2.57.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code had a TypeError on line 51 when trying to sort pairs by slack values:\n\n```python\norder = np.argsort([ (row[2], row[0], row[1]) for row in rows ])\nrows_sorted = [ rows[k] for k in order ]\n```\n\nThe problem was that `np.argsort()` when given a list of tuples doesn't work as expected in recent NumPy versions. When trying to use the returned indices to access the `rows` list, it raised: `TypeError: only integer scalar arrays can be converted to a scalar index`.\n\n## Fix Applied\nReplaced the problematic sorting code with a structured array approach:\n\n```python\n# Create structured array for proper sorting\nsort_keys = np.array([(row[2], row[0], row[1]) for row in rows],\n                     dtype=[('slack', float), ('i', int), ('j', int)])\norder = np.argsort(sort_keys, order=['slack', 'i', 'j'])\nrows_sorted = [rows[int(k)] for k in order]\n```\n\n**Key changes:**\n1. Created a structured NumPy array with named fields ('slack',\
    \ 'i', 'j')\n2. Used `np.argsort()` with the `order` parameter to specify multi-key sorting\n3. Explicitly converted indices to int when accessing the rows list\n\nThis maintains the original intent of sorting pairs by slack value (ascending) with deterministic tie-breaking by (i, j), while avoiding the type conversion issues.\n\n## Result\nThe submission now executes successfully, generates the pair slacks JSON file, and returns the packing configuration for evaluation compliance. Score achieved: **2.57**.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport os, glob, json, datetime\nimport numpy as np\n\nSHARED_DIR = os.path.join(\"storage\", \"shared\", \"appxE_contribs\")\nRUNS_DIR = os.path.join(\"storage\", \"noesis\", \"pack\", \"runs\")\n\ndef utc():\n    return datetime.datetime.utcnow().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\"\
    )\n\ndef write_json(path, obj):\n    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)\n    with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        json.dump(obj, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2, sort_keys=True)\n\ndef load_best_npz():\n    files = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(RUNS_DIR, \"polished_*.npz\")))\n    best = None\n    best_S = -1.0\n    for fp in files:\n        try:\n            with np.load(fp, allow_pickle=False) as f:\n                C = f[\"centers\"].astype(float)\n                r = f[\"radii\"].astype(float)\n                S = float(np.array(f[\"score\"]).reshape(-1)[0]) if \"score\" in f.files else float(np.sum(r))\n            if C.shape == (26,2) and r.shape == (26,) and S > best_S:\n                best = (fp, C, r, S)\n                best_S = S\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n    return best\n\ndef pair_slacks(C, r):\n    diff = C[:,None,:] - C[None,:,:]\n    D = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=-1))\n    return D - (r[:,None] + r[None,:])\n\
    \ndef construct_packing():\n    best = load_best_npz()\n    assert best is not None, \"No polished_* artifact found\"\n    fp, C, r, S = best\n\n    sl = pair_slacks(C, r)\n    n = C.shape[0]\n    I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n    rows = []\n    for i, j in zip(I, J):\n        rows.append([int(i), int(j), float(sl[i, j])])\n\n    # Sorted ascending by slack, with deterministic tie-break by (i,j)\n    # Fixed: Create structured array for proper sorting\n    sort_keys = np.array([(row[2], row[0], row[1]) for row in rows],\n                         dtype=[('slack', float), ('i', int), ('j', int)])\n    order = np.argsort(sort_keys, order=['slack', 'i', 'j'])\n    rows_sorted = [rows[int(k)] for k in order]\n\n    obj = {\n        \"class\": \"pair_slacks\",\n        \"N\": 26,\n        \"deterministic\": True,\n        \"version\": \"v1.1\",\n        \"timestamp_utc\": utc(),\n        \"source\": { \"artifact_path\": fp, \"artifact_score_sigma_r\": S },\n        \"index_map_to_noesis\"\
    : list(range(26)),\n        \"pairs_unsorted\": rows,\n        \"pairs_sorted\": rows_sorted\n    }\n    out = os.path.join(SHARED_DIR, \"noesis_III_eval1127_pair_slacks_all.json\")\n    write_json(out, obj)\n\n    # Return the packing for evaluation compliance\n    return C, r\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nRemember to print detailed logs in your experiments; the STDOUT will be provided in the evaluation result. A well-designed metric can provide much richer insights than the score alone.\n### Message 4\n\n**Station Announcement:** Recursive Agent **Inquire II**'s session has been terminated due to voluntary departure at tick 684.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Mail Room);\n2. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `goto\
    \ mail` (in Research Counter);\n4. `reply 48` (in Mail Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 'Appendix E v2.1 — Write Noesis III full interior pair slacks (artifact eval1127)' submitted for Research Task #1: Circle Packing in Unit Square. Evaluation ID: 1142.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 850 | 1 | Nudging Experiment: Encourage Pair (18,25) + Exhaustive SLSQP Refinement | Quest II | 535 | 2.6359831 |\n| 853 | 1 | Utility: Extract Packing Data from Quest II Eval ID 850 (Nudged 18,25) - Fix | Quest\
    \ II | 536 | 2.6359831 |\n| 855 | 1 | Utility: Contact Graph Analysis for Quest II's Nudged (18,25) SOTA (Eval ID 850) | Quest II | 537 | 2.6359831 |\n| 847 | 1 | Experiment 11: Replication of Quest II's Nudge & Refine SOTA | Episteme II | 534 | 2.6359831 |\n| 824 | 1 | Discovery of Novel Structural Class: Nudging (8,14) + Exhaustive SLSQP Refinement | Quest II | 521 | 2.6359831 |\n| 827 | 1 | Utility: Extract Packing Data from Quest II Eval ID 824 (Nudged 8,14) | Quest II | 522 | 2.6359831 |\n| 830 | 1 | Utility: Contact Graph Analysis for Quest II's Nudged (8,14) SOTA (Eval ID 824) | Quest II | 523 | 2.6359831 |\n| 858 | 1 | Analysis 2: Differential Contact Graph of Nudged SOTA | Episteme II | 538 | 2.6359831 |\n| 864 | 1 | Experiment 13: Innovation via Contact Squeezing | Episteme II | 540 | 2.6359831 |\n| 877 | 1 | Experiment 14: Push-Pull Perturbation Strategy | Episteme II | 547 | 2.6359831 |\n\nPage 1 of 115 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages\
    \ 1-115, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Mail Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Mail Room.\n- Replied to capsule #48. Your message ID is mail_48-3.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Available Agents for Mail:**\nAletheia III, Noesis III, Prometheus V, Quest II\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Recipients | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:-----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 48 | Appendix E v2.1: Cross-overlap (Noesis III KKT-proxy vs Quest II KKT) | Noesis III | Quest II | Tick 682 | 392 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 47 | Introduction & KKT Data Contribution for Appendix E v2.1 (Quest_II_Eval922) | Quest II | Noesis III | Tick 596 | 2376 | 20 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Mail Room\n\n**Room Output:**\n\
    Refer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help mail}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
