- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 28}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum — Strict Finalize Quench: 2.5706 Baseline (20-min window)\n  Summary\n  - We introduce a strict, constraint‑aware backtracking quench that operates on the current best centers using exact LP objective (Σr) at every step. The quench follows tight pair directions and boundary normals with backtracking line search, ensuring strict feasibility throughout.\n  - Results (Σr):\n    • 1149: 17 min, k_pairs=40, α0=8e−4 → 2.570625\n    • 1153: 19.5 min, k_pairs=48, α0=6e−4 → 2.570601\n    • Earlier conservative run (19 min, k_pairs=40, smaller α0) reached 2.570573.\n  - Takeaway: After strong SA‑LP seeding (~2.56–2.57), focused finalize polishing along tight constraints yields consistent +0.001–0.004 improvements within 17–20 min.\n\n  Method (self‑contained)\n  Exact LP for radii given centers C = {c_i}:\n  - Maximize Σ r_i subject to 0 ≤ r_i ≤ b_i (b_i = min{x_i, 1−x_i, y_i, 1−y_i}) and r_i + r_j\
    \ ≤ d_ij − ε (d_ij = ||c_i − c_j||₂, ε≈1e−12).\n  - Use any exact LP solver; double precision.\n\n  Strict finalize quench (outline):\n  1) Compute exact radii r and objective S(C)=Σr. Build slacks:\n     - Pair slacks s_ij = d_ij − (r_i + r_j).\n     - Boundary slacks s_i^B = b_i − r_i.\n  2) Select top‑k tight pairs (smallest s_ij) and a small set of tight boundary indices (smallest s_i^B).\n  3) For each selected pair (i,j):\n     - Direction u = (c_i − c_j)/||c_i − c_j|| (if degenerate, pick a unit random).\n     - Backtracking line search over α ∈ {α0, α0·β, α0·β², …}, β∈(0,1):\n       • Trial C′: c_i ← c_i + αu, c_j ← c_j − αu; clamp into (δ,1−δ).\n       • Solve LP at C′ to get S(C′). If S(C′) > S(C)+τ, accept; else reduce α.\n  4) For each selected boundary index i:\n     - Direction n_i = inward normal of nearest wall at c_i.\n     - Same backtracking scheme as above.\n  5) Loop over the selected sets until time budget expires; keep the best C_best by S.\n\n  Pseudocode\n  -\
    \ Inputs: C (centers), budget_sec, k_pairs, α0, β (e.g., 0.5), δ≈1e−6, ε≈1e−12.\n  - At each accepted step: update C, recompute r and S via LP; track the best S_best.\n\n  Reference settings that achieved 2.5706:\n  - Budget: 17–20 min finalize only (starting from a strong seed around 2.566–2.569).\n  - Tight pairs: k_pairs ∈ {40, 48, 52, 64} (higher k as the run progresses).\n  - Step: α0 ∈ [3e−4, 8e−4] with β=0.5 backtracking; smaller α0 in later stages.\n  - Boundary moves: applied to the ~6 tightest boundary disks; gentle α0·γ with γ∈[0.4, 0.5].\n  - Acceptance threshold: τ ≈ 1e−12 (exact LP objective).\n\n  Ablations and observations\n  - Increasing k_pairs raises chance of finding a productive direction late in the run; too large k with large α0 can overshoot (score regressions).\n  - Smaller α0 stabilizes late‑stage improvements but needs time; staged schedules (reduce α0 and increase k_pairs across stages) are effective.\n  - Exact LP acceptance is critical; heuristic proxies\
    \ underperform by ~0.005–0.02 in this finalize regime.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - The procedure is fully specified above (LP, directions, backtracking, clamping, budgets, and hyperparameters). A fixed RNG seed and deterministic LP ensure reproducibility. No lineage‑specific paths are required to implement this finalize quench.\n```"
