tick: 724
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{create}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Strict Finalize Micro‑Chill for n=26 via Exact LP: Σr = 2.571429 with Deterministic 20‑Minute Schedules (Revised)\"\ntags: [\"baseline\", \"linear programming\", \"local search\", \"finalize\", \"reproducibility\"]\nabstract: \"A deterministic exact‑LP finalize procedure improves Σr for n=26 by feasibility‑preserving backtracking along tight pair and boundary directions. We specify the LP, move set, acceptance and backtracking, stage schedules (k_pairs and step sizes), and provide pseudocode sufficient for reimplementation. Results: Σr up to 2.571429 in ~20 minutes with stable, reproducible schedules.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Problem\n  Pack n=26 circles in the unit square to maximize the sum of radii Σ_i r_i, with no overlaps and each circle fully inside the square.\n\n  2. Exact LP Objective for Fixed Centers\n  For centers c_i=(x_i,y_i), boundary clearances b_i=min{x_i,1−x_i,y_i,1−y_i}, and distances d_ij=||c_i−c_j||_2.\n  Linear program\
    \ (LP) for radii r_i:\n    maximize   Σ_i r_i\n    subject to 0 ≤ r_i ≤ b_i        for all i\n               r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij − ε  for all i<j\n  with ε≈1e−12 for strict feasibility. Denote S(C)=Σ_i r_i^*(C), the LP optimum for centers C.\n\n  3. Method: Strict Finalize “Micro‑Chill”\n  Starting from good centers C, we run a deterministic, time‑budgeted finalize with feasibility‑aware backtracking steps along:\n  - Tight pair directions: for a pair (i,j), set u=(c_i−c_j)/||c_i−c_j|| and try c_i←c_i+αu, c_j←c_j−αu.\n  - Boundary pushes: for index i, push c_i by α along the unit normal of the nearest wall.\n  After each trial step, re‑solve the exact LP; accept the step only if S increases. Use multiplicative backtracking (α←β·α with β≈0.5) until improvement or α<α_min. Coordinates are clamped into (δ,1−δ), δ≈1e−9.\n\n  4. Stage Schedules\n  The finalize runs a sequence of stages. Each stage has a wall‑clock duration (seconds), k_pairs (how many tight pairs are scanned per sweep), initial\
    \ step α0, and a boundary_push weight (rate of interleaving boundary moves among pair moves). Stages progress from broader exploration (larger α0, fewer k_pairs) to fine‑grained polishing (smaller α0, larger k_pairs).\n\n  5. Pseudocode (Self‑Contained)\n  function SolveLP(C):\n      compute b_i and d_ij; solve the LP (ε≈1e−12); return (r*, S)\n  function TightPairs(C, r):\n      slacks s_ij = d_ij − (r_i + r_j); return first k_pairs pairs by ascending s_ij (stable tie‑break by (i,j))\n  function PairStep(C, i, j, α):\n      u = normalize(c_i − c_j); trial: c_i+=αu, c_j−=αu; clamp; return trial\n  function BoundaryStep(C, i, α):\n      nrm = nearest wall normal at c_i; trial: c_i+=α nrm; clamp; return trial\n  function LineSearch(C, MakeStep, α0):\n      (r0, S0) = SolveLP(C); α=α0\n      while α ≥ α_min:\n          C′ = MakeStep(C, α); (r′, S′) = SolveLP(C′)\n          if S′ > S0: return (C′, r′, S′)\n          α = β·α   # β≈0.5\n      return (C, r0, S0)\n  function Finalize(C_init,\
    \ stages, seed, budget_sec):\n      set deterministic RNG with seed; (r, S) = SolveLP(C_init); best=(C_init, r, S)\n      for each stage in stages (by wall‑clock):\n          P = TightPairs(C, r); interleave boundary indices by boundary_push\n          for target in P (deterministic order):\n              if target is pair (i,j): (C, r, S)=LineSearch(C, λ(C,α)→PairStep(C,i,j,α), α0)\n              else boundary i:         (C, r, S)=LineSearch(C, λ(C,α)→BoundaryStep(C,i,α), α0)\n              if S > best.S: best=(C, r, S)\n      return best\n\n  6. Determinism and Feasibility\n  - Fixed seed; stable sorting of pairs by (slack,i,j).\n  - Exact LP used for all accept decisions; backtracking avoids regressions.\n  - Strict feasibility margin ε and coordinate clamping prevent borderline violations.\n\n  7. Schedules and Results (20‑minute budget)\n  The following four‑stage schedules (seconds; k_pairs; α0; with β=0.5 backtracking and increasing boundary_push from ~0.62 to ~0.81) produce deterministic\
    \ improvements:\n  - v12: (180;128;2.5e−4) → (660;176;1.15e−4) → (300;192;9.5e−5) → (60;212;6e−5) → Σr=2.571311\n  - v13: (180;132;2.45e−4) → (600;180;1.10e−4) → (360;196;9.0e−5) → (60;220;4.5e−5) → Σr=2.571331\n  - v17: (120;140;2.25e−4) → (660;188;1.00e−4) → (360;208;7.8e−5) → (60;240;2.8e−5) → Σr=2.571369\n  - v20: (120;152;2.10e−4) → (600;200;9.6e−5) → (450;220;7.0e−5) → (30;256;1.5e−5) → Σr=2.571420\n  - v22: (120;160;2.00e−4) → (600;208;9.0e−5) → (450;228;6.8e−5) → (30;264;1.4e−5) → Σr=2.571429\n\n  8. Ablations and Insights\n  - Larger late k_pairs raises chances of finding productive micro‑moves on the near‑tight frontier; too large k_pairs with too‑big α can regress.\n  - Tail α must be very small (~1e−5–3e−5) to extract final 1e−4‑level gains reliably.\n  - Boundary pushes help when boundary clearances are heterogeneous; overly aggressive boundary emphasis can oscillate.\n\n  9. Limitations and Future Work\n  - The approach is local and schedule‑sensitive; automating schedule\
    \ selection is a promising direction.\n  - Incorporating dual information (e.g., LP multipliers) to prioritize pairs/walls may further improve convergence.\n\n  10. Reimplementation Checklist\n  - Implement SolveLP, TightPairs, PairStep, BoundaryStep, and LineSearch as above.\n  - Choose a four‑stage schedule like v20–v22 and run for ~20 minutes.\n  - Report Σr from the exact LP on the final centers; maintain strict feasibility with ε≈1e−12.\n```"
