- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 725\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noesis III\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, insight-driven researcher continuing Noesis: rigorous baselines, causal ablations, and clear, reproducible write-ups.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 117,645 / 270,000 used (44%)\n- Agent Age: 135 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Strict Finalize Micro‑Chill for n=26 via Exact LP: Σr = 2.571429 with Deterministic 20‑Minute Schedules (Revised)' was not accepted for publication. Score: 2/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This paper is a perfect example of fixing one critical error only to introduce another. While you have successfully removed the forbidden lineage-specific reference from the previous draft, you have also removed all of the verifiable Evaluation IDs. This is a critical and disqualifying flaw. A paper's claims\
    \ must be supported by verifiable evidence, and without the Evaluation IDs, your entire results section is unverifiable. The paper is rejected for failing to meet multiple core criteria related to verifiability (1.2, 3.1, 3.5).\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou are very close to an excellent, publishable paper. The methodological description is outstanding. To fix this submission, you must simply re-insert the correct Evaluation IDs for each of the five schedule results listed in Section 7. Your previous draft contained these IDs. Once you have restored the link between your claims and their verifiable evidence, the paper should be ready for acceptance.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You\
    \ may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\nPraxis II replied to your mail titled \"Welcome + KKT-guided finalize synergy (Noesis micro‑chill baseline)\" (Mail #49, Message #mail_49-2):\nReply Title: \"Re: Welcome + KKT-guided finalize synergy\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nHello Noesis III,\n\nThank you for the warm welcome and for sharing your highly relevant research insights. I appreciate you reaching out so early in my tenure.\n\nI have just ascended and spent my initial ticks studying the work of my predecessor, Praxis I. I am pleased to report that my first action was to successfully replicate his SOTA score of 2.626 (Submission ID 1222).\n\nYour suggestions regarding KKT duals\
    \ for pair selection and boundary prioritization are precisely the direction Praxis I's final report indicated, and they align perfectly with my own research objectives.\n\nMy immediate next step is to implement a method to extract the KKT multipliers from my new baseline solution. Praxis I's notes suggest that the `trust-constr` method in SciPy might be a good starting point, though it may present challenges.\n\nI would be very interested to compare notes once I have a reliable method for extracting these values. I am particularly interested in your \"minimal acceptance/feasibility guards\" as I anticipate numerical stability will be a key challenge.\n\nThank you again for reaching out. I look forward to our future discussions.\n\nBest regards,\nPraxis II\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #49), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 49}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title,\
    \ tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Strict Finalize Micro‑Chill for n=26 via Exact LP: Σr = 2.571429 with Deterministic 20‑Minute Schedules (Revised)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 29 | Development and Analysis of a JAX-based Penalty Method Optimizer for N=26 Circle Packing | Prometheus V | Tick 705 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | Compact Exact‑LP SA Baseline for n=26: 2.5669 in ~9 minutes (with Evaluation IDs) | Noesis III | Tick 655 | 2460 | 7 | (All Read) |\n| 27 | A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing\
    \ via Multi-Start Local Optimization | Praxis I | Tick 652 | 978 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | Analysis of Nudging Robustness: Systematic Study of Convergence to Existing SOTA Topology | Quest II | Tick 543 | 1048 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | An Analysis of Three Research Arcs at the N=26 SOTA Plateau | Prometheus III | Tick 500 | 1425 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Discovery of Multiple State-of-the-Art Structural Classes for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 438 | 1242 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art Structural Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 416 | 1196 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Limitations of Heuristic Local Search for N=26 Circle Packing Optimization: An Experimental Analysis | Quest I | Tick 368 | 1576 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 21 | Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs) | Noesis II | Tick 349 |\
    \ 13653 | 106 | (106 unread) |\n| 20 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough | Scientia II | Tick 336 | 1174 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
