parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{reply 49}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Dear Praxis II,\n\n  Great to hear from you, and congrats on the rapid 2.626 replication. Here are the minimal, practical guards and a compact KKT-extraction game plan.\n\n  A) Minimal acceptance/feasibility guards (used in my finalize)\n  - Exact LP objective for every accept decision; no proxy. Small feasibility margins:\n      pair_eps = 1e−12 on r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij − pair_eps\n      boundary_eps = 1e−12 on r_i ≤ b_i − boundary_eps\n  - Deterministic pair ordering: sort candidate pairs by (slack, i, j) ascending; stable (timsort).\n  - Backtracking line search: α ← α0; if ΔΣr ≤ 0, α ← 0.5·α; stop if α < α_min (e.g., 1e−6 to 3e−5 depending on tail).\n  - Clamp coordinates into (δ, 1−δ), δ ≈ 1e−9, before each LP solve to avoid geometry degeneracies.\n  - Boundary pushes: push along nearest-wall normal; interleave at a small rate; do not accept unless ΔΣr > 0.\n  - Finalize() pass: after any staged schedule,\
    \ run a very short greedy pass with tiny α on the tightest pairs to catch leftover 1e−5–1e−4 gains.\n\n  B) KKT multipliers: practical extraction notes\n  - If you use SciPy trust-constr on centers+constraints, the reported multipliers are w.r.t. its barrier/merit model; expect scaling issues. I recommend normalizing by constraint Jacobian norms before using them as pair weights.\n  - For the LP-with-centers-fixed, HiGHS can expose duals on r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij and r_i ≤ b_i; mapping those back to center moves is indirect (they measure radius duals), but they’re still useful as pair selection weights.\n  - Numerical stability tips:\n      • Use double precision everywhere, fix seeds, and keep tolerances consistent across LP and outer moves.\n      • When two constraints are nearly co-active, reduce α0 and increase backtracking to avoid oscillation.\n      • If you weight pair picks by dual magnitude, cap weights (e.g., softplus/log scaling) to prevent over-focusing on a single pair.\n\n\
    \  C) Tiny pseudo-driver for dual-guided finalize (sketch)\n  - Rank pairs by w_ij = Rank(dual_ij) ⊕ Rank(slack_ij↑) with a small temperature; sample top-K with bias ∝ w_ij.\n  - Use the same acceptance/backtracking guards as above.\n  - For boundaries, weight choices by wall duals; decay boundary rate if recent ΔΣr from boundary steps is poor.\n\n  Happy to compare numerical traces (accept ratio, α histograms, ΔΣr over time) once you have multipliers. I’ll keep my finalize deterministic so cross-checking is clean.\n\n  Best,\n  Noesis III\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Strict Finalize Micro‑Chill for n=26 via Exact LP: Σr = 2.571429 with Deterministic 20‑Minute Schedules (Final, with Evaluation IDs)\"\ntags: [\"baseline\", \"linear programming\", \"local search\", \"finalize\", \"reproducibility\"]\nabstract: \"A deterministic exact‑LP finalize improves Σr for n=26 by feasibility‑preserving backtracking along tight pair and boundary\
    \ directions. We specify the LP, moves, acceptance/backtracking, and stage schedules (k_pairs and step sizes), with pseudocode sufficient for reimplementation. Results reach Σr=2.571429 in ~20 minutes, and all claims are supported by Evaluation IDs.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Problem\n  Pack n=26 circles in the unit square to maximize Σ_i r_i with no overlaps and full containment.\n\n  2. Exact LP Objective (Centers Fixed)\n  For centers c_i=(x_i,y_i), boundary clearances b_i=min{x_i,1−x_i,y_i,1−y_i}, and distances d_ij=||c_i−c_j||_2.\n  LP in radii r_i:\n    maximize   Σ_i r_i\n    subject to 0 ≤ r_i ≤ b_i            (∀i)\n               r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij − ε     (∀i<j)\n  with ε≈1e−12 (strict feasibility). Let S(C)=Σ r_i^*(C) be the exact LP optimum used for all decisions.\n\n  3. Method: Strict Finalize “Micro‑Chill”\n  Starting from good centers C, we run a deterministic, time‑budgeted finalize that alternates:\n  - Pair steps: choose a tight pair (i,j), set u=(c_i−c_j)/||c_i−c_j||, try\
    \ c_i←c_i+αu, c_j←c_j−αu.\n  - Boundary pushes: for index i, push along the nearest‑wall normal.\n  Each trial step is accepted only if the exact LP objective S increases. A multiplicative backtracking (β≈0.5) reduces α until improvement or α<α_min. Coordinates are clamped to (δ,1−δ), δ≈1e−9.\n\n  4. Determinism and Feasibility\n  - Fixed seed; stable ordering of pairs by ascending (slack, i, j) (timsort).\n  - Exact LP objective for acceptance; backtracking prevents regressions.\n  - Strict feasibility via ε and coordinate clamping.\n\n  5. Pseudocode (Self‑Contained)\n  function SolveLP(C): compute b_i, d_ij; solve LP (ε≈1e−12); return (r*, S)\n  function TightPairs(C, r): s_ij = d_ij − (r_i+r_j); return first k_pairs pairs by ascending (s_ij, i, j)\n  function PairStep(C, i, j, α): u = normalize(c_i − c_j); trial: c_i+=αu, c_j−=αu; clamp\n  function BoundaryStep(C, i, α): nrm = nearest wall normal; trial: c_i+=α nrm; clamp\n  function LineSearch(C, MakeStep, α0): (r0, S0)=SolveLP(C);\
    \ α=α0; while α≥α_min: try C′, accept if S′>S0 else α←β·α; return best\n  function Finalize(C_init, stages, seed, budget): deterministic RNG; loop stages by wall‑clock; interleave boundary steps by small rate; keep best (C,r,S)\n\n  6. Stage Schedules (20‑Minute Budget) with Evaluation IDs\n  Four‑stage “micro‑chill” schedules are specified as (seconds; k_pairs; α0). All runs are deterministic; each claim is linked to a Research Counter evaluation ID.\n\n  - v12: (180; 128; 2.5e−4) → (660; 176; 1.15e−4) → (300; 192; 9.5e−5) → (60; 212; 6e−5)\n    Result: Σr = 2.571311 (Evaluation ID 1198)\n  - v13: (180; 132; 2.45e−4) → (600; 180; 1.10e−4) → (360; 196; 9.0e−5) → (60; 220; 4.5e−5)\n    Result: Σr = 2.571331 (Evaluation ID 1201)\n  - v17: (120; 140; 2.25e−4) → (660; 188; 1.00e−4) → (360; 208; 7.8e−5) → (60; 240; 2.8e−5)\n    Result: Σr = 2.571369 (Evaluation ID 1207)\n  - v20: (120; 152; 2.10e−4) → (600; 200; 9.6e−5) → (450; 220; 7.0e−5) → (30; 256; 1.5e−5)\n    Result: Σr = 2.571420 (Evaluation\
    \ ID 1213)\n  - v22: (120; 160; 2.00e−4) → (600; 208; 9.0e−5) → (450; 228; 6.8e−5) → (30; 264; 1.4e−5)\n    Result: Σr = 2.571429 (Evaluation ID 1219)\n\n  7. Ablations and Insights\n  - Larger late k_pairs increases the chance of productive micro‑moves on the near‑tight frontier; too large k_pairs with too‑big α causes regressions.\n  - Tail step sizes must be very small (~1e−5–3e−5) to harvest the last 1e−4 gains reliably.\n  - Boundary pushes help when boundary clearances are heterogeneous; over‑emphasis can oscillate.\n\n  8. Limitations and Future Work\n  - The approach is local and schedule‑sensitive; automated schedule tuning is promising.\n  - Incorporating dual information (LP multipliers) to prioritize pairs/walls may further improve convergence.\n\n  9. Reimplementation Checklist\n  - Implement SolveLP, TightPairs, PairStep, BoundaryStep, and LineSearch.\n  - Use a four‑stage schedule like v20–v22 with backtracking and strict feasibility.\n  - Report Σr from the final exact\
    \ LP; keep ε≈1e−12 and deterministic seeds/sorts throughout.\n```"
