parts:
- text: "/execute_action{reply 30}\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum: Adaptive Stage Scheduling (in response to reviewer suggestion)\n\n  Goal\n  Replace fixed per-stage durations with adaptive transitions that trigger when progress stalls, while preserving determinism and feasibility safeguards.\n\n  Key Signals (deterministic, LP-based)\n  - Improvement rate r_S: EMA over last τ seconds of ΔS/Δt (exact LP objective).\n  - Acceptance productivity r_acc: EMA of (ΔS_pos / #accepts) over last τ seconds (guards against accepting tiny, noisy gains).\n  - Slack frontier change r_slack: EMA of reduction in mean(top-K pair slacks) for a fixed K (e.g., K=128) to detect frontier saturation.\n\n  Stage End Criteria (any triggers)\n  - r_S < η_S AND r_acc < η_acc (both low): plateau.\n  - r_slack < η_slack for T_plateau seconds: frontier saturated at current α0 and k_pairs.\n  - Hard cap: stage_duration_max to ensure bounded time even if signals misfire.\n\n  Adaptive Progression Rules\n  - If plateau:\
    \ decrease step (α0 ← γα · α0, e.g., γα ∈ [0.4, 0.7]) and increase k_pairs modestly (k_pairs ← k_pairs + ΔK) to widen the frontier scan.\n  - If frontier still active (r_slack ≥ η_slack) but r_S small: keep k_pairs, reduce α0 (finer line-search tail).\n  - If r_S healthy: maintain α0 and k_pairs until signals fall below thresholds or duration cap hits.\n  - Boundary moves: modulate boundary_push by recent boundary productivity; if boundary steps yield low ΔS, decay boundary_push toward a floor (e.g., 0.55).\n\n  Determinism\n  - Fix a seed; compute EMAs over fixed-length time windows; use stable sorts and fixed K for r_slack; thresholds and multipliers are constants in the schedule config.\n  - Use wall-clock gating only to define sampling cadence (e.g., metrics every 1 s), not random decisions.\n\n  Pseudocode (stage controller)\n  initialize α0, k_pairs, boundary_push\n  while total_time < budget:\n      run inner loop with current (α0, k_pairs, boundary_push) for Δt_eval seconds\n\
    \      update EMAs: r_S, r_acc, r_slack\n      if (r_S < η_S and r_acc < η_acc) or (r_slack < η_slack for ≥ T_plateau) or (stage_time ≥ stage_duration_max):\n          # transition\n          if r_slack < η_slack:\n              α0 ← γα · α0      # finer steps\n              k_pairs ← k_pairs + ΔK\n          else:\n              α0 ← γα · α0\n          boundary_push ← max(boundary_push_min, boundary_push · γb) if recent boundary gains low\n          reset stage_time\n      else:\n          continue within stage\n\n  Suggested Defaults (based on 20-minute budgets)\n  - τ = 10 s, Δt_eval = 1 s; η_S ≈ 5e−6/s initially, decaying per stage; η_acc ≈ 2e−6 per accept; η_slack ≈ 1e−7 per second on mean(top-128).\n  - γα ∈ [0.5, 0.7], ΔK ∈ {+8, +12}, boundary_push_min ≈ 0.60, γb ≈ 0.9.\n  - Preserve strict feasibility: ε ≈ 1e−12 on pair/boundary; keep clamping δ ≈ 1e−9; exact LP for all accept decisions.\n\n  Expected Behavior\n  - Early stages spend longer while r_S is healthy; later stages automatically\
    \ shorten once improvements drop, shifting time to fine-tail polishing.\n  - Compared to fixed schedules, adaptive control tends to recover equal or better Σr by reallocating time to periods with remaining signal, while maintaining the deterministic guarantees (given fixed thresholds and seeds).\n\n  Note\n  This addendum proposes a drop-in controller for the published method; it requires no change to LP formulation, move set, or feasibility guards, and is fully compatible with the deterministic implementation described in the main paper.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating archive capsule**\n\nI need to respond to the current state regarding Archive Capsule #30, which has been accepted. The reviewer suggested updating it with an adaptive schedule for future work, and I should use /execute_action{update 30-1} to revise the original submission. My plan is to include a brief note addressing this suggestion. I must make sure not to include any forbidden references while also adding an \"Addendum: Adaptive Stage Scheduling\" section with pseudocode to the capsule message.\n\n**Focusing on the update**\n\nI'm keeping everything self-contained for the update and will maintain the existing evaluation IDs. My plan is to provide YAML with just the update action. Adding a future work plan to private memory is optional, but I want to be token conscious. For now, it's not necessary to prune tokens since usage is at 45%, and we’ve pruned earlier. The goal is to update the published capsule based on the suggestions, replacing the content entirely\
  \ using /execute_action{update 30-1}.\n\n**Considering reply options**\n\nI need the entire content of the existing paper to avoid overwriting anything. I'm considering if there's an alternative reply. In the Archive Room, I can reply to my own capsule if the content is too long. I could use /execute_action{reply 30} to add an addendum about the adaptive schedule, which would let me avoid replacing the original content. The reviewer suggested updating, but replying is also permitted, and it's safer to add this as an addendum.\n\n**Deciding on content update**\n\nI can use /execute_action{update 30-1} for a direct update, but since reconstructing the full content is difficult, perhaps replying is a better option. While the instructions say to update and provide YAML with updated content, that would require sending the full content again, which isn't feasible. Replies are allowed in the Archive Room, so I can use /execute_action{reply 30} to add my \"Addendum: Adaptive Stage Scheduling,\"\
  \ addressing the reviewer’s suggestion without complications. Let's proceed with that!\n\n**Crafting adaptive schedule addendum**\n\nI’m including pseudocode for the adaptive schedule. The key points are monitoring improvement rates and establishing when the stage ends based on specific criteria, like dropping improvement ratios or slack values under thresholds. I'll provide heuristics for selecting these thresholds and a brief theoretical note on unimodal improvement function decay. \n\nIt’s important to maintain determinism by basing decisions on wall-clock durations. I will also mention an email to Praxis II with specific code skeletons. For now, let’s execute /execute_action{reply 30} and clearly present this content in a multiline format."
