  \ constraints to hold. Alternatively, I can ensure that `topk_slack_mean` uses the current radius from the safe solver. By modifying it to accept the current radius as optional, I can maintain safety and potentially fix any pairwise violations.\n\n**Updating adaptive finalize process**\n\nI want to make sure that when returning from `adaptive_finalize`, I also finalize using `AQ.finalize_feasible` if it's available. I need to ensure that the boundary clamp maintains radii within safe limits. So, I'll update `active_quench_adaptive.py` and the recipe info accordingly. I'm also considering running a new evaluation version. We have to stay within the allowed actions and monitor token usage. For modifications, I propose replacing `topk_slack_mean` with `slack_frontier`, which will use a safe solver when appropriate, ensuring we finalize with the right margins to fix the overlaps issue.\n\n**Implementing finalize feasibility**\n\nI realize we’re unsure if `AQ.solve_radii_lp_safe` is actually\
  \ exported, but since `active_quench` uses it, it could still be useful. I’m planning to implement a `finalize_feasible` function to manage pair slacks and boundaries effectively. The algorithm involves computing the boundary and clamping radii, ensuring non-negativity through a series of iterations. If there are negative slacks, I’ll compute contributions for pairs and distribute adjustments accordingly. I’ll aim for around four iterations to ensure everything stabilizes while keeping the complexity manageable.\n\n**Finalizing and submitting code changes**\n\nAfter calling `finalize`, I need to check if pair slacks are all above -1e-12; if not, I'll slightly shrink radii or apply several passes. I’m implementing a `finalize_feasible` function to manage radii and pair slacks efficiently. The function will handle updating values for radii based on constraints, and I’ll also update `topk_slack_mean` to work with this new function. \n\nLastly, I’m preparing to submit the updated code for\
  \ “Adaptive strict finalize v2” with proper titles and tags, ensuring clarity and consistency while avoiding dependency issues."
